WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.952
A full house looks like success.

00:00:02.952 --> 00:00:07.932
But does a bigger crowd always mean a better business?

00:00:07.932 --> 00:00:13.375
Let us explore a miniature concert, using invented numbers.

00:00:13.525 --> 00:00:19.403
At a ticket price of twenty, one hundred and fifty guests attend.

00:00:19.403 --> 00:00:21.754
Revenue is three thousand.

00:00:21.754 --> 00:00:28.989
After production, venue and per-person costs, profit is seven hundred and fifty.

00:00:29.139 --> 00:00:31.985
Raise the ticket price to thirty.

00:00:31.985 --> 00:00:34.572
Now one hundred guests attend.

00:00:34.572 --> 00:00:38.712
Revenue stays at three thousand, but costs fall.

00:00:38.712 --> 00:00:41.213
Profit rises to one thousand.

00:00:41.363 --> 00:00:46.823
Now rent a thousand-place venue without changing interest.

00:00:46.823 --> 00:00:51.435
The crowd stays the same, while venue costs rise.

00:00:51.435 --> 00:00:55.295
The event loses twelve hundred and fifty.

00:00:55.445 --> 00:01:00.669
More capacity is not the same as more demand.

00:01:00.669 --> 00:01:03.572
Try your own assumptions.

00:01:03.572 --> 00:01:09.841
Follow the money, and remember: revenue is not profit.
